<div class="article-layout">
<main id="main-content" tabindex="-1"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/gta5/">GTA 5</a><span>/</span><span>Guide</span></div><h1>How To Host Public Sessions with Scooby (BE Disabled)</h1><p class="article-intro">GTA 5 · Scooby Wiki</p><div class="guide-card" id="host-public">
                <h2 class="sr-only">How To Host Public Sessions with Scooby (BE Disabled)</h2>
                <p>Getting a public lobby to fill up is not just luck - it comes down to a handful of factors you can actually control. This guide covers what makes players join your session and what to do when nobody shows up.</p>
                <div class="requirements-box" id="premium-hosting">
                    <h4>Premium: you must remain the session host</h4>
                    <p>This hosting guide is for <strong>Premium with BattlEye disabled</strong>. Premium lets you host public lobbies and play with friends, but you must <strong>remain the session host at all times</strong> to avoid being desynced after about <strong>3–5 minutes</strong> as a non-host.</p>
                    <p><strong>Premium Plus</strong> supports <strong>BattlEye enabled</strong> and lets you play as either the host or a non-host. The free version does not support public lobbies.</p>
                    <p><a href="/guides/gta5/free-setup/#online-access">Compare Free, Premium, and Premium Plus online access →</a></p>
                </div>
                <p>For the Premium hosting steps below, launch GTA 5 with <strong>BattlEye disabled</strong> in the Rockstar Launcher settings. Premium Plus users playing with BattlEye enabled do not need to follow this host-only setup.</p>

                <p>There are <strong>four factors</strong> behind a lobby that actually fills: your NAT type, your aim type, the region and time you host in, and a fair amount of luck.</p>

                <h3 id="topic-14">1. NAT Type</h3>
                <p>Your NAT type affects how easily other players can connect to your lobby.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
<strong>Open NAT</strong> - best results for hosting</li>
                    <li>
<strong>Moderate NAT</strong> - usually works fine</li>
                    <li>
<strong>Strict NAT</strong> - may reduce player joins and connectivity</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Important things to know:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>NAT type is normally controlled by your <strong>router and your ISP</strong>, not by the game.</li>
                    <li>Some tools and scripts offer NAT spoofing, which presents a different NAT type to GTA Online. Results vary a lot and it does not work the same for every player.</li>
                    <li>If you consistently struggle to attract players, check your NAT type and confirm your ISP is not putting you behind CGNAT (see below).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 id="topic-15">2. Aim Type</h3>
                <p>Your aim setting decides which pool of players can even match with you.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
<strong>Free Aim</strong> - largest player pool on PC, attracts the most players</li>
                    <li>
<strong>Partial Aim Assist</strong> - still a decent number of players</li>
                    <li>
<strong>Full Aim Assist</strong> - smallest player pool on PC, expect fewer joins</li>
                </ul>
                <p>For the highest chance of filling a lobby, <strong>start with Free Aim</strong>.</p>

                <h3 id="topic-16">3. Region and Timing</h3>
                <p>Timing matters as much as the region itself. Aim for hours when people are:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Getting home from work</li>
                    <li>Finishing school</li>
                    <li>Already gaming during the evening</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Good windows to try:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>North America, evenings</li>
                    <li>Europe, evenings</li>
                    <li>Weekends beat weekday mornings by a wide margin</li>
                </ul>
                <p>If one region is quiet, switch to another and try again.</p>

                <h3 id="topic-17">4. Luck</h3>
                <p>Sometimes everything is set up perfectly and the lobby still will not fill. You can host a session that fills instantly, then host the exact same session 30 minutes later and get nobody. GTA Online matchmaking is unpredictable, so persistence is part of the process.</p>

                <h3 id="topic-18">Bonus: Check for CGNAT</h3>
                <p>Some ISPs place customers behind <strong>Carrier Grade NAT (CGNAT)</strong>, which shares one public IP between many customers. This can hurt your connectivity, your matchmaking and your hosting reliability.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Contact your ISP</li>
                    <li>Ask whether your connection is behind CGNAT</li>
                    <li>Request a public IPv4 address if they offer one</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 id="topic-19">Bonus: Use a VPN</h3>
                <p>A VPN can sometimes improve how visible you are in matchmaking. Best practice:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Connect to a VPN server in a <strong>densely populated area</strong>
</li>
                    <li>Match your VPN region to the audience you are trying to attract</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Locations players commonly report good results with: <strong>New York, Chicago, Los Angeles, London, Frankfurt</strong>. Results vary, but busy metro areas generally give better lobby population.</p>

                <div class="requirements-box">
                    <h4>Checklist - you have done everything right if:</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You have Premium and remain the session host</li>
                        <li>Session advertising is enabled</li>
                        <li>You are on Open or Moderate NAT</li>
                        <li>You picked the right Aim Type (Free Aim fills fastest)</li>
                        <li>You chose an active region and a busy time of day</li>
                        <li>You confirmed you are not behind CGNAT</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 id="topic-20">Still Alone?</h3>
                <p>
<strong>Be patient.</strong> Try different combinations, restart the session, switch regions and keep experimenting. An empty lobby after one attempt does not mean your setup is wrong.</p>

                <h3 id="topic-21">If All Else Fails - Get Friends to Join First</h3>
                <p>A lobby that already has a few players inside attracts far more players than an empty one. Once the first few people arrive, GTA Online matchmaking tends to do the rest. You can also turn on <strong>Player Magnet</strong> in the Network tab to help pull players in.</p>

                <p>In short: hosting is a combination of <strong>NAT type, aim type, region, ISP quality and a little bit of luck</strong>.</p>
            </div><nav class="article-pagination" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/guides/gta5/money/"><span>← Previous guide</span><strong>Money Making Methods</strong></a><a href="/guides/gta5/troubleshooting/"><span>Next guide →</span><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></a></nav><footer class="wiki-footer">
